WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.667 --> 00:00:03.234
♪♪

00:00:03.234 --> 00:00:04.734
-I often say in the body

00:00:04.734 --> 00:00:07.634
of "Corrections"
that I stand corrected.

00:00:07.634 --> 00:00:12.400
I've been told
I should say, "I sit corrected."

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.634
Because I never stand
on this show.

00:00:15.634 --> 00:00:20.834
I, of course, used to stand
when we started this show,

00:00:20.834 --> 00:00:22.934
but I had to stop when --

00:00:22.934 --> 00:00:26.801
there's no easy way
to say this --

00:00:26.801 --> 00:00:28.767
everybody was
a real dick about it.

00:00:33.434 --> 00:00:36.901
The vice president lives
at the Naval Observatory,

00:00:36.901 --> 00:00:38.667
not The White House,
which is something

00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:42.934
I'm pretty sure Obama
had to tell Biden every day.

00:00:45.801 --> 00:00:48.534
[ As Obama ] Shouldn't you be,
uh, getting home?

00:00:52.901 --> 00:00:55.267
[ Normal voice ] We did
a "Surprise Inspection"

00:00:55.267 --> 00:00:57.367
of our writers,
the segment on our show

00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:01.801
where we read some of
the bad jokes they submitted.

00:01:01.801 --> 00:01:03.868
Matt Goldich
wrote a joke about Pepsi

00:01:03.868 --> 00:01:07.501
no longer producing Sierra Mist

00:01:07.501 --> 00:01:13.133
and how that was sad because it
was the main ingredient

00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:15.300
in Sierra Mistu.

00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:20.567
This was the art card for that.

00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:22.734
And then people said,
"In Sierra Mist,

00:01:22.734 --> 00:01:25.901
the bottle is green,
not the drink."

00:01:25.901 --> 00:01:31.000
And that is the only problem
with that joke.

00:01:36.067 --> 00:01:39.133
There was also a joke
about the show "Wednesday."

00:01:39.133 --> 00:01:41.734
The punch line was,
"Here's The Thing..."

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:45.467
And then we showed a picture
of the character Thing.

00:01:45.467 --> 00:01:50.200
That joke worked,
got a proper applause break.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:53.467
Now, I'm not sure if people
actually liked the joke

00:01:53.467 --> 00:01:57.834
or if it was just so good
compared to what was around it.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.067
You know, if you're
in the desert long enough,

00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:05.767
you'll drink your own piss.

00:02:09.901 --> 00:02:11.901
It doesn't mean it's refreshing.

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:16.868
There's a reason
Pepsi stopped producing it.

00:02:21.634 --> 00:02:24.901
But whatever reason,
be it the quality

00:02:24.901 --> 00:02:26.968
or the surroundings,
people did genuinely

00:02:26.968 --> 00:02:28.167
like that joke.

00:02:28.167 --> 00:02:30.901
I will concede the point.

00:02:30.901 --> 00:02:35.067
And multiple people then,
in turn, made this point.

00:02:35.067 --> 00:02:36.834
And now I'm quoting someone.

00:02:40.067 --> 00:02:42.901
They said,
"You didn't say who wrote --"

00:02:42.901 --> 00:02:44.300
Because we do call out
the writers

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.868
after their
"Surprise Inspection."

00:02:46.868 --> 00:02:48.934
"You didn't say who wrote
the 'Wednesday' Thing joke.

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:50.067
You do this all the time.

00:02:50.067 --> 00:02:51.868
When the joke gets a laugh,

00:02:51.868 --> 00:02:56.567
you move on without saying
the writer's name."

00:02:56.567 --> 00:02:59.300
That's true.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:07.033
We made a joke
that the Mars candy corporation

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:09.767
should have been inspired by
current events and created

00:03:09.767 --> 00:03:14.634
a new mascot, a gray M&amp;M,
who carries around

00:03:14.634 --> 00:03:15.834
classified documents.

00:03:15.834 --> 00:03:17.934
I forgot. You guys, I forgot.

00:03:17.934 --> 00:03:21.534
1997, there was a gray M&amp;M.

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:25.200
There was a whole
marketing event created by

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:28.934
M&amp;M with the imposter M&amp;M,
who was gray,

00:03:28.934 --> 00:03:31.834
and if you found a gray M&amp;M,
you'd get $1 million.

00:03:31.834 --> 00:03:34.167
Now, I know some people
who found him,

00:03:34.167 --> 00:03:36.934
and they did not turn it in
for $1 million,

00:03:36.934 --> 00:03:38.934
because -- I don't know
what it was.

00:03:38.934 --> 00:03:41.033
I don't know
if it was the gray dye

00:03:41.033 --> 00:03:42.734
they used or what.

00:03:42.734 --> 00:03:46.601
Because the high
when you took one of those

00:03:46.601 --> 00:03:50.567
was out of this world.

00:03:50.567 --> 00:03:53.868
You remember '97, Shoemaker?

00:03:53.868 --> 00:03:57.434
Poppin' grays?

00:03:57.434 --> 00:03:58.834
I mean...

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:05.834
We'd be listening to The Verve
or The Verve Pipe.

00:04:05.834 --> 00:04:10.801
What a time.
What a time for Verve bands.

00:04:10.801 --> 00:04:14.400
'90s. It was a cornucopia

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.167
of bands with Verve
in the title.

00:04:16.167 --> 00:04:18.000
And we had Korn.

00:04:20.534 --> 00:04:25.200
For the life of me,
I cannot remember.

00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:29.234
What made me think
that we were wise

00:04:29.234 --> 00:04:31.200
and we'd never compromise?

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:39.601
Tell you this, though.

00:04:39.601 --> 00:04:47.667
Married now, three kids,
bills to pay.

00:04:47.667 --> 00:04:52.367
If I get a gray M&amp;M now,
I turn it in for the money.

00:04:56.467 --> 00:04:59.801
I got called out
on a mispronunciation again,

00:04:59.801 --> 00:05:01.133
which is bad,
because I've already

00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:05.901
gotten in enough trouble
with the knitting community.

00:05:05.901 --> 00:05:09.467
I said this was

00:05:09.467 --> 00:05:13.267
someone holding an
"I Love Wally" mug

00:05:13.267 --> 00:05:14.567
at crotch level.

00:05:14.567 --> 00:05:16.801
It's pronounced "croch."

00:05:25.133 --> 00:05:27.234
We mentioned Sir Isaac Newton.

00:05:27.234 --> 00:05:30.200
I said he would ignore -- He was
so into the idea of gravity,

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:32.400
he would ignore it if a
naked lady fell into his lap.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:37.567
A lot of you said
he probably would ignore it.

00:05:37.567 --> 00:05:42.167
It is speculated
that he died a virgin

00:05:42.167 --> 00:05:43.534
because he thought --

00:05:43.534 --> 00:05:45.200
One person --
Only one person said this.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.234
I didn't do any digging.

00:05:47.234 --> 00:05:49.133
I don't find
the guy that interesting.

00:05:51.868 --> 00:05:53.367
That he was a virgin
because he thought

00:05:53.367 --> 00:05:55.000
that would give him immortality.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:56.434
And, in a way, you know what?
He's right.

00:05:56.434 --> 00:05:58.601
Because here we are.
Obviously, he died.

00:05:58.601 --> 00:06:01.334
Still talking about him,
still saying his name.

00:06:01.334 --> 00:06:02.400
God bless him.

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.300
While all
his other friends were,

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:08.734
to use the parlance of the time,
out chasin' tail,

00:06:08.734 --> 00:06:12.501
he knew to be
sitting in a field,

00:06:12.501 --> 00:06:14.100
watching --
in an orchard or whatever --

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:17.767
just making sure he was
the guy who was there

00:06:17.767 --> 00:06:19.367
when the apple came down.

00:06:25.033 --> 00:06:29.567
We talked about my mistake
two weeks back

00:06:29.567 --> 00:06:31.968
referring to the
Reillys as the O'Reillys.

00:06:31.968 --> 00:06:34.501
There was an incident
I did not mention last week

00:06:34.501 --> 00:06:37.567
because I had been advised by
my legal team

00:06:37.567 --> 00:06:43.801
to let it play out...

00:06:43.801 --> 00:06:47.200
in the courts.

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:52.400
So, after I made the mistake --

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:56.567
incorrectly referred
to the Reillys

00:06:56.567 --> 00:06:58.667
as the O'Reillys, I found this
in my dressing room.

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:06.634
Not by itself -- with this.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:13.501
And you know what I did?

00:07:13.501 --> 00:07:17.968
Went to H.R. immediate--
right away, reported it,

00:07:17.968 --> 00:07:20.467
which is what they told us
to do this week...

00:07:24.234 --> 00:07:28.634
in our Respect in the Workplace
seminar.

00:07:28.634 --> 00:07:29.968
And if that doesn't
sound familiar,

00:07:29.968 --> 00:07:33.400
Wally, it's because they said it
in the first 15 minutes.

00:07:37.067 --> 00:07:43.234
You -- I wish you could
all see Wally roll into

00:07:43.234 --> 00:07:47.467
an all-staff meeting dead last,

00:07:47.467 --> 00:07:51.634
not a care in the world,

00:07:51.634 --> 00:07:54.300
sunglasses inside.

00:07:55.734 --> 00:07:58.701
Just sits down, all his bags.

00:08:01.434 --> 00:08:04.467
Wally don't need no preamble.

00:08:09.267 --> 00:08:10.501
You know what you are good at,
though, Wally?

00:08:10.501 --> 00:08:12.534
Scanning in the QR code.

00:08:18.634 --> 00:08:20.667
Point is, i call H.R., say,

00:08:20.667 --> 00:08:22.801
"I got this threatening note
in my dressing room."

00:08:22.801 --> 00:08:25.000
And they say, you know,
"Do you have proof it's,

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:26.234
you know,
it's one of the Reillys?"

00:08:26.234 --> 00:08:28.033
And I'm like, "Who else
would leave it there?"

00:08:28.033 --> 00:08:30.234
And they were like, "Without
proof, we can't do anything."

00:08:30.234 --> 00:08:32.267
And I'm like, "Who's going to
stand up to this

00:08:32.267 --> 00:08:34.968
reign of terror, you know?"

00:08:34.968 --> 00:08:36.901
And then she says something,
the H.R. person.

00:08:36.901 --> 00:08:38.300
Always very nice,
until this thing.

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:39.567
She goes, "I'm gonna tell
you something.

00:08:39.567 --> 00:08:41.033
If you ever repeat it,
I'm going to

00:08:41.033 --> 00:08:42.734
disavow I ever said it.

00:08:42.734 --> 00:08:45.901
Let it go.

00:08:45.901 --> 00:08:48.167
It's not worth it.
Let it go."

00:08:52.067 --> 00:08:57.234
And I'm not saying
she's a Reilly,

00:08:57.234 --> 00:09:00.167
but when I said, "I'm going
to kick it up the ladder,"

00:09:00.167 --> 00:09:03.033
she called me in "eedgit."

00:09:08.767 --> 00:09:13.033
I've been complaining about
people misusing our P.O. Box

00:09:13.033 --> 00:09:15.300
to send Mac Tonight
merchandise and crafts.

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:17.501
I'm happy to report
this has slowed down.

00:09:17.501 --> 00:09:20.801
I received a very cool gift
from a jackal

00:09:20.801 --> 00:09:25.667
with the request that I show it
first and then read the note.

00:09:25.667 --> 00:09:30.267
This is really very cool.

00:09:30.267 --> 00:09:32.367
Take a look.

00:09:32.367 --> 00:09:35.567
And I'm guessing,
because a jackal sent it,

00:09:35.567 --> 00:09:38.634
I think I can guess what it is.

00:09:41.234 --> 00:09:45.200
"Dear, Seth, your dog Frisbee
is with the angels now.

00:10:01.367 --> 00:10:04.067
Not. It's a skull that...

00:10:05.167 --> 00:10:07.334
Someone named Mason.

00:10:07.334 --> 00:10:10.534
Thank you, Mason.

00:10:10.534 --> 00:10:15.000
Said they got it -- Poached it?
Got it. No.

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:20.634
It's a black-backed-jackal
skull.

00:10:20.634 --> 00:10:22.567
And this is cool.

00:10:22.567 --> 00:10:25.267
It has -- The tape
is actually there for a reason.

00:10:25.267 --> 00:10:28.767
I guess even after
a jackal dies,

00:10:28.767 --> 00:10:31.234
they don't stop [bleep] yapping.

00:10:36.534 --> 00:10:38.067
Someone also wrote --
Because I would think

00:10:38.067 --> 00:10:40.467
this would, like, pair very well
with everything out here,

00:10:40.467 --> 00:10:44.167
but I guess someone just wrote a
very simple note that said,

00:10:44.167 --> 00:10:47.100
"You have too much [bleep]
on your desk."

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:50.367
It might be time to
thin that out.

00:10:50.367 --> 00:10:55.367
Oh, I sang a few lines
from "Bad, Bad Leroy Brown."

00:10:55.367 --> 00:10:58.634
Some people were
unfamiliar with the song,

00:10:58.634 --> 00:11:00.968
asked who
the original singer was.

00:11:00.968 --> 00:11:03.100
"Jim Crotch."

00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:04.701
Croce?

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:13.067
The Clapper uses two claps,
not one.

00:11:13.067 --> 00:11:15.667
Did a thing where
Mike Pence was running,

00:11:15.667 --> 00:11:17.501
and the lights would go off
every time he clapped

00:11:17.501 --> 00:11:18.701
and then back on.

00:11:18.701 --> 00:11:21.167
It's two claps, not one.
Bunch of you knew that.

00:11:21.167 --> 00:11:23.801
And it's just weird
how many of the 20-somethings

00:11:23.801 --> 00:11:25.834
who I assume make up
the lion's share of our

00:11:25.834 --> 00:11:27.434
audience would know that.

00:11:33.968 --> 00:11:37.834
I read the comments from the
previous week's "Corrections,"

00:11:37.834 --> 00:11:39.200
newest first.

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:41.100
So I'm going in reverse order.

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:48.901
And we told -- made a prop joke
about Amelia Earhart.

00:11:48.901 --> 00:11:51.934
And, so, I read a comment
from someone that said,

00:11:51.934 --> 00:11:53.734
"Thank you for the prop joke.

00:11:53.734 --> 00:11:55.334
I might be, but I hope not,

00:11:55.334 --> 00:11:57.901
the only pilot who watches
'Corrections.'"

00:11:57.901 --> 00:12:00.567
The very next one I read,
which would have been

00:12:00.567 --> 00:12:04.000
the previous comment --
Same person.

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:06.868
Their previous comment before
the one I just read you was,

00:12:06.868 --> 00:12:09.667
"I honestly don't know why
I watch this."

00:12:12.434 --> 00:12:15.400
That must have been
so amazing for you.

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:17.200
So upset that you
leave a comment,

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:19.968
and just seconds later,

00:12:19.968 --> 00:12:23.634
I make a joke specific
to your profession.

00:12:23.634 --> 00:12:24.934
What a day.

00:12:30.501 --> 00:12:35.868
We made a lot of flubs regarding
the animal kingdom this week...

00:12:37.968 --> 00:12:40.567
which means it's time
for our brand-new segment,

00:12:40.567 --> 00:12:42.033
"Animal Flubs."

00:12:42.033 --> 00:12:45.067
♪ Animal flubs, animal flubs ♪

00:12:45.067 --> 00:12:47.701
♪ It's time to take a look
at some animal flubs ♪

00:12:47.701 --> 00:12:50.701
♪ From the birds to the bees
to the baby bear cubs ♪

00:12:50.701 --> 00:12:53.200
♪ It's time for animal flubs ♪

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:55.868
♪ And I know
you don't need to say

00:12:55.868 --> 00:12:59.367
"baby" before
you say "bear cub" ♪

00:12:59.367 --> 00:13:04.734
♪ Because in that case,
cub means baby ♪

00:13:04.734 --> 00:13:07.701
♪ But when you're writing a song
on a Thursday afternoon

00:13:07.701 --> 00:13:10.801
when you really should be
working on "A Closer Look" ♪

00:13:10.801 --> 00:13:13.701
♪ Sometimes, your guy
might cut a few corners

00:13:13.701 --> 00:13:17.067
in order to make
the rhyme scheme work ♪

00:13:17.067 --> 00:13:19.667
♪ Animal flubs, animal flubs ♪

00:13:19.667 --> 00:13:22.634
♪ I hope you're all ready
for some animal flubs ♪

00:13:22.634 --> 00:13:25.434
♪ Call all your
pals, buddies, and buds ♪

00:13:25.434 --> 00:13:27.334
♪ It's time for animal flubs ♪

00:13:27.334 --> 00:13:29.834
F.

00:13:29.834 --> 00:13:31.534
L.

00:13:31.534 --> 00:13:32.801
U.

00:13:32.801 --> 00:13:34.434
B.

00:13:34.434 --> 00:13:37.000
S.

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:40.667
♪ Are the five letters ♪

00:13:40.667 --> 00:13:44.601
♪ You use ♪

00:13:44.601 --> 00:13:47.334
♪ To spell "flubs" ♪

00:13:47.334 --> 00:13:48.868
♪ Animal flubs ♪

00:13:48.868 --> 00:13:51.634
You're right --
a cappella was better.

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:02.000
We mentioned beef prices rising,

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:04.567
but we showed a picture
of a dairy cow.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:07.901
That's an animal flub.

00:14:10.734 --> 00:14:12.367
This is an
African pygmy hedgehog,

00:14:12.367 --> 00:14:15.567
the one we
commonly have as pets.

00:14:15.567 --> 00:14:18.767
A European hedgehog
would be brown all over

00:14:18.767 --> 00:14:20.868
without any white.

00:14:20.868 --> 00:14:22.834
But they're both delicious.

00:14:26.634 --> 00:14:29.434
We did a joke about a bird
getting in the house,

00:14:29.434 --> 00:14:34.801
and we were told
the Photoshop here --

00:14:34.801 --> 00:14:38.234
the size ratio is way off.

00:14:38.234 --> 00:14:42.667
This Eurasian tree sparrow
got way scaled up.

00:14:42.667 --> 00:14:44.400
That's an animal flub.

00:14:47.501 --> 00:14:49.000
Trump mentioned
you'd go to jail

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:52.701
for five years
if you killed a bald eagle.

00:14:52.701 --> 00:14:55.100
But someone said
that was only true when

00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:56.767
bald eagles were endangered.

00:14:56.767 --> 00:14:59.767
And due
to conservation efforts,

00:14:59.767 --> 00:15:01.501
they've been making a comeback.

00:15:01.501 --> 00:15:05.968
So bad time to be a bald eagle
with gambling debts.

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:14.167
Koalas -- Oh, we had a joke.

00:15:14.167 --> 00:15:16.133
Here's the art card we used.

00:15:16.133 --> 00:15:21.934
There was a radioactive capsule
went missing in Australia,

00:15:21.934 --> 00:15:23.501
and we said,
"We think we found it."

00:15:23.501 --> 00:15:26.934
And someone pointed out
that the capsule

00:15:26.934 --> 00:15:32.701
was found in Western Australia,
where there are no koalas.

00:15:32.701 --> 00:15:35.734
Bad animal flub.

00:15:35.734 --> 00:15:39.067
They said
we should have used a quokka.

00:15:39.067 --> 00:15:42.167
I had no idea what a quokka was.

00:15:42.167 --> 00:15:45.100
I was a little afraid to look,
because Australia has some

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:49.000
weird-ass-looking animals,
but it was not that weird.

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:51.167
Let's take a look at
some video of a quokka.

00:15:51.167 --> 00:15:53.334
[ Man screams ]
Seven days.

00:15:59.834 --> 00:16:02.334
[ Laughs ]

00:16:02.334 --> 00:16:04.534
Oh, this has been
"Animal Flubs."

00:16:04.534 --> 00:16:05.968
♪ Animal flubs ♪

00:16:09.033 --> 00:16:11.033
[ Gasps ]

00:16:19.667 --> 00:16:20.868
[ Shudders ]

00:16:39.868 --> 00:16:41.267
One second.

00:16:55.934 --> 00:16:57.267
Can we get a single, Alex?

00:17:00.801 --> 00:17:03.300
See me next week.
[ Laughter ]

